{"term": "جارية", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "jāriya", "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "concubinage", "concept": "concubinage", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 15, "technical": 25, "recognition_pct_current": 72, "recognition_pct_peak": 95, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "A female slave who served as a concubine in classical Islamic society, legally available for her owner's sexual use under the institution of milk al-yamīn (right-hand possession).", "english_term": "concubine / slave girl", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From the root j-r-y (to flow/run); a jāriya was legally distinct from a wife (zawja) but could be freed and elevated in status by bearing her master's child (umm al-walad). The institution was abolished across the Arab world in the 19th–20th centuries but the term persists in classical literature, Quranic exegesis, and historical fiction."}
