{"term": "مفضوح", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "mafḍūḥ", "category": "honor_shame_economy", "subcategory": "sexual_scandal", "concept": "slut_shaming", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "archaic", "crude": 45, "technical": 10, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "One who has been publicly exposed and disgraced, especially for sexual misconduct; a person whose shameful acts have been brought to light before the community.", "english_term": "disgraced / publicly outed for sexual wrongdoing", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Passive participle of faḍaḥa (to expose/scandalize). Gender-neutral grammatically but applied with heavier social consequences when directed at women in traditional Arabic contexts. Maps only approximately to slut_shaming — mafḍūḥ applies to any public sexual or moral scandal, not specifically to promiscuity stigma. Central concept in honor-shame discourse."}
{"term": "التشهي", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "al-tashahhī", "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "sexual_desire", "concept": "lust", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 20, "technical": 15, "recognition_pct_current": 50, "recognition_pct_peak": 68, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Sexual craving or appetite; the intense desire for carnal gratification.", "english_term": "lust / sexual craving", "english_translation_quality": "direct", "notes": "Masdar of Form V verb tashahhā (to crave), from root sh-h-w (appetite/desire). The simpler root-form shahwa (carnal desire) is far more common in everyday and religious discourse; al-tashahhī is the elevated literary variant. Appears in classical erotic manuals such as The Perfumed Garden. The definite article in the input (al-) indicates nominal/citation form."}
{"term": "الاستلذاذ", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "al-istildhādh", "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "erotic_pleasure", "concept": "eroticism", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 15, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 38, "recognition_pct_peak": 55, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "The deliberate act of seeking out and savoring erotic or sensual pleasure; the cultivation of carnal delight as an end in itself.", "english_term": "erotic enjoyment / savoring pleasure", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Masdar of Form X verb istaladhdha (to seek/find delectable), from root l-dh-dh. More formal and rarer than taladdudh; carries a connotation of intentional, connoisseur-like pursuit of pleasure rather than mere experience of it. Found primarily in classical medical and erotic literature; unlikely to be produced in contemporary speech."}
{"term": "مؤانسة", "lang": "ar", "script": "arab", "romanization": "muʾānasa", "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "intimate_companionship", "concept": "intimate_physical_play", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 10, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 42, "recognition_pct_peak": 60, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Pleasurable intimate companionship; in classical mujūn literature, the shared enjoyment of drink, music, and sexual pleasure in refined erotic company.", "english_term": "intimate companionship / pleasurable company", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From ʾanisa (to be intimate/familiar with); the root ʾ-n-s connotes warmth, familiarity, and comfort. Sexual connotations are entirely context-dependent: in religious or neutral contexts the word simply means socializing or conviviality; in mujūn poetry it implies erotic companionship. The registry match intimate_physical_play captures the physical register but loses the social and intellectual dimensions of the classical concept."}
